# Single source of truth for the tool pins used by the example/parity scripts.
# Sourced by: co-noir/co-noir/examples/{compare.sh,compare_mpc.sh,compile.sh}
# and test_vectors/noir/compile.sh; quoted by co-noir/co-noir/Readme.md.
# Bump the pin HERE (one-line diff) instead of editing each script.
NARGO_VERSION=1.0.0-beta.22
BARRETENBERG_VERSION=5.0.1
# THE parity oracle: bb source-built at $BARRETENBERG_VERSION (PROVENANCE.txt
# lives beside the binary; a source-built bb prints a placeholder --version).
# Prebuilt release binaries are NOT trustworthy oracles: a release binary can
# predate the tag's tree (the 5.0.0-rc.2 lesson) — build from source and
# verify provenance every time (project-docs ch. 12 §3 first gotcha).
#
# ⚠ NIGHTLY/RELEASE TAG NAMES ARE NOT RELIABLE VERSION IDENTIFIERS ACROSS
# REPOS (project-docs ch. 19 §1): the previous pin here, "5.0.0-nightly.
# 20260708", was never actually the public aztec-packages tag of that name —
# it was a private-repo commit sharing the same nightly label from a
# different (later) merge-train branch, which for the UltraHonk-critical
# source paths turned out to already equal v5.0.1 exactly (verified by a
# byte-identical from-source rebuild + an empty tree-diff to v5.0.1 across
# every proving-scheme-relevant directory — ch. 19 §1.2). Bumping the pin
# string here to "5.0.1" is therefore not a content change, only a
# correction of the label to something unambiguous (an actual public release
# tag) going forward. Do not assume a nightly tag name uniquely identifies a
# commit without checking — re-verify tree/commit identity every sync, not
# just the label.
BARRETENBERG_BINARY_DEFAULT="$HOME/.bb-target-src-v5.0.1/bb"
# Prior pin's oracle (kept on disk, not deleted): ~/.bb-target-src/bb
# (PROVENANCE.txt there names private-repo commit 289aaec1e027, which this
# v5.0.1 oracle's source tree is identical to for every UltraHonk path).
